WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.803
ROBERT BALLARD: The
Titanic was really

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:09.242
a cover for a highly
classified military operation

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:12.645
to investigate two nuclear
submarines that we lost

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:16.216
during the Cold War
with all hands, the USS

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:19.652
Thresher and the USS Scorpion.

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:22.956
They ironically turned
out to be on either side

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:25.291
of where the Titanic was lost.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:28.695
And quite honestly, had
that not been the case,

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:32.065
you wouldn't be looking at the
guy that found the Titanic.

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:35.268
The Navy wanted
complete, 100% photo

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:37.971
documentation of
both wreck sites

00:00:38.037 --> 00:00:40.573
and to answer some questions.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.209
What are the nuclear
reactors doing?

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:44.744
What's the status
of nuclear weapons?

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:47.547
Is there any evidence the
Soviets have been there.

00:00:47.614 --> 00:00:51.451
Well, I said to both the
Secretary of the Navy John

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:55.588
Lehman and Admiral Thunman who
was my boss as a Naval officer,

00:00:55.655 --> 00:01:00.760
well, what if we use the
Titanic as the cover?

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:03.129
N. RONALD THUNMAN: I thought
it was a cockamamie story.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:04.664
I mean, find the Titanic.

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:06.699
ROBERT BALLARD: And I said,
well, it'd be a great cover,

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:07.700
wouldn't it?

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:09.169
Finally he said, thumbs up.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.204
N. RONALD THUNMAN: I told
him, do whatever you want,

00:01:11.271 --> 00:01:12.739
just don't spend any more money.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.344
ROBERT BALLARD: Boy,
did I work fast.

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.413
We pretty well knew what
happened to the Thresher.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.582
We mapped her a year earlier.

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:27.187
They knew very little
about the Scorpion.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:30.423
It took me about a week
to get there and map it.

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:35.829
We mapped it extensively.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:39.599
And there was no evidence
of human remains.

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:42.869
I mean, that would have
been tough, but no.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:46.339
The reactors were intact,
so that was good because.

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:49.742
N. RONALD THUNMAN: There were no
other indications of something

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:52.779
amiss other than the
submarine had gone down

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:54.481
and it had imploded.

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.549
ROBERT BALLARD: As soon
as the Navy was satisfied,

00:01:56.616 --> 00:02:00.019
we headed northwest
for the Titanic.

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.555
I had 12 days to hunt.

00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:05.458
But I had learned something from
the Thresher and the Scorpion

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:08.628
that I couldn't tell anyone,
because both submarines

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:12.165
imploded before they
came to the bottom.

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:16.135
So on their way down [clap]
the pressure holes went.

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:21.074
And that implosive event
is a gigantic explosion.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.578
And it just blew the
submarines apart.

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:28.948
But when I was mapping
it, we realized

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:33.052
that the heavy stuff went down
like a bowling ball, reactors--

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.454
boom.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:38.558
But then the lighter stuff
was a big, long line.

00:02:38.625 --> 00:02:41.694
And I went, didn't the
Titanic do the same thing?

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:46.165
So let's not look for the
big pieces of Titanic,

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:47.767
because they're only that big.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:51.271
Let's look for the debris, which
meant I could space my search

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:54.440
lines much wider apart.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.175
NARRATOR: But
Titanic did not lie

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.444
where she was thought to be.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:02.315
Days of futile
search dragged on.

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:05.952
INTERVIEWER: How close
were you to failure?

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.487
ROBERT BALLARD: Well I had--

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:12.025
I was reaching the point of
where I thought we'd failed.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.260
I thought we had.

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:15.695
And I was going--

00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:18.698
I went back to my room,
and we had the watch going.

00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:20.233
It was a mid-watch.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.702
Everything happens on the
midnight watch, 12:00 to 4:00.

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.137
I don't know why.

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:26.873
Everything important occurs
right around midnight.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:39.819
Wreckage Bingo, yeah.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:41.187
FEMALE: Did you find it?

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:44.057
MALE: [inaudible]
somebody [inaudible]..

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.992
And I went in,
and there it was.

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:47.660
I mean, the boiler
was on the screen.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.062
Oh, I love it I love it.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:52.899
I'll be [bleep].

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:55.368
The sucker exists.

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:57.136
Hey, does Kathy
got the champagne?

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.105
N. RONALD THUNMAN: We
were jumping up and down.

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:02.609
We were patting one
another on the back.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:07.380
And then someone looked at the
clock in the command center

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:09.849
and said, she sinks
in 20 minutes.

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:10.950
It was 2:00 in the morning.

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:13.286
She sank at 2:20.

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:17.223
And that innocent
comment just grounded us.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:21.761
And we realized, you
have finally put to rest

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:24.697
were these souls were lost.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.165
And everyone filed out.

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:31.070
And it was a moment of
silence, because we were there.

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:32.038
And that was pretty--

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:33.206
pretty powerful.

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:44.350
[music playing]

